Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: 'Material Adverse Effect' means any event, change, effect, circumstance, or development that, individually or in combination with any other events, changes, effects, circumstances, or developments, (i) has a material adverse effect on the financial condition, business, assets, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) prevents or materially delays the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. However, in the case of clause (i) only, none of the following shall be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect or be taken into account: (a) changes in economic conditions, financial markets, or political conditions that do not disproportionately affect the Company relative to other industry participants; (b) changes in laws or regulations applicable globally or nationally; (c) natural disasters or pandemics, unless such events disproportionately affect the Company compared to others in the industry; (d) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement; (e) any failure by the Company to meet any internal or external projections, forecasts, or estimates of revenue or earnings, except that any underlying causes of such failures may be considered unless otherwise excluded by this definition. Additionally, for both clauses (i) and (ii), any adverse effects resulting from the Company's compliance with the terms of this Agreement or actions taken with the consent of the Parent shall not be considered. (Pages 23-24)\nAnswer:
Choices:

